win32u: keep a concurrent invalidation when applying a visible region

update_visible_region() fetches the region from the server and then calls
set_visible_region(), which also clears the DC's dirty flag. When another
thread moves or resizes the window between those two steps, its
invalidate_dce() sets the flag on the DC in use and the stale region then
clears it again. The cached DC keeps the old region until the next unrelated
invalidation.
The Power BI frame receiver paints the WebView host from its own thread
every 16 ms while the UI thread resizes that host. On a tiling compositor a
short-lived second window (halve, then restore) left the receiver clipped to
the half-width client area: the right half of Desktop stayed blank.
Both callers that fetch a region clear the flag before they fetch
(NtUserGetDCEx through DCHF_VALIDATEVISRGN, update_dc directly), so the extra
reset is only ever a lost update. The third caller, release_dce(), applies an
empty region without fetching; a flag left set there is cleared by the next
NtUserGetDCEx, which refetches for a released DC in any case. Leave the flag
alone; the next drawing call refetches. The cost is at most one extra region
fetch per racing invalidation.
Wine-derived change: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

--- a/dlls/win32u/clipping.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/clipping.c
@@ -217,7 +217,9 @@
     NtGdiOffsetRgn( hrgn, -vis_rect->left, -vis_rect->top );
 
     if (dc->hVisRgn) NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( dc->hVisRgn );
-    dc->dirty = 0;
+    /* Do not clear dc->dirty here: this region was fetched earlier, and another
+     * thread may have moved the window and flagged the DC since. The callers
+     * that fetch a region already clear the flag before they do. */
     dc->attr->vis_rect = *vis_rect;
     dc->device_rect = *device_rect;
     dc->hVisRgn = hrgn;
